1
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Thanks Pat, thanks to the Royal Institution for having me back, this is one of my favorite places to come visit

2
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
and I thought that I would in the tradition of Michael Faraday and Humphrey Davy,

3
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
and all the greats who've stood more or less in this place

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Begin the lecture by doing an experiment.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Now I'm a theoretical physicist. I'm not an experimenter, so don't get your hopes up too high

6
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
But I would like to do an experiment that illuminates the fundamental nature of motion.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Ok so you see here. We have an object. It's a book you can buy it at the finest book stores everywhere

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,000
and we're going to observe what happens to the object.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Nothing much happens to the object is the short answer to it

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,000
it just sits there, but I can if I push on the object get it moving and

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
then if I stop pushing, it stops moving -- the nature of motion there being demonstrated to you right there.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
So if you were the kind of systematic thinker that let's say Aristotle was,

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
you would go from a demonstration like that to a very deep picture of how the world works at a fundamental level. 

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
You would say that there is something called the natural state for the book to be in,

15
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
which is just sitting there motionless.

16
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
You would say that if you don't do anything an object stays in its natural state.

17
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
But if you apply a force to it -- an impetus -- you can change its natural state.

18
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
You can cause it to start moving and you notice when I then stopped pushing

19
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:51,000
it stopped. It returns to its natural state.

20
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:58,000
So you invent what philosophers would call an ontology or a metaphysics or a fundamental view

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
of what the world is made of. It's made of things that have natural states.

22
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
And if they're not staying stationary and unchanging in their natural states,

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
it is because something is changing them. When there is motion there is a Mover.

24
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
And you can go from this simple idea to a theory of physics as Aristotle did

25
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and you can in fact go beyond that to a theory of metaphysics that explains not just the motion of things

26
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:26,000
but more or less how everything changes and transforms at all.

27
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
You can even go all the way back -- you can say well, okay

28
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
this thing is moving because this other thing is pushing it. But what causes that other thing to be moving?

29
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
If you trace the chain of motion and movers backward you eventually

30
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
would need to reach an Unmoved Mover and thereby prove the existence of God

31
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
without ever leaving the lecture hall right here.

32
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
This way of thinking is not only a systematization of some very simple physics experiments you can do,

33
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
it also sort of accords well with our everyday experience.

34
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
When you see something moving or not moving, it's because something is moving it or not moving it.

35
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:11,000
So there became a philosophical tradition, which tried to go from the physics of it to a deeper understanding

36
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,000
claiming that everything that Happens -- whether it's motion or otherwise -- Happens for a Reason.

37
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
This is not just a bumper sticker you can buy,

38
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
this is a very venerable philosophical maxim known as the Principle of Sufficient Reason.

39
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,000
So here you have Aristotle, Spinoza followed up with his version of the Principle of Sufficient Reason

40
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and then Leibniz on the right German philosopher who also invented Calculus

41
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:45,000
They all put forward this idea that we can understand how the world works at a deep level by providing

42
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
explanations for everything we see in it. Nothing happens randomly,

43
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:52,000
nothing "just happens" -- there's always a Purpose, a Cause, a Reason Why Things Happen.

44
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:01,000
You might be forgiven for thinking that the ultimate purpose was ever more grandiose hairstyles for professional philosophers

45
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Even if Leibniz sort of had some artificial help there in in in his coiffeur

46
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
The problem is that this is not right.

47
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
The problem is that the world does not work this way at a fundamental level.

48
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
There's two problems -- one is it's not right; the other is despite the fact that it's not right, we haven't abandoned it yet.

49
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
So we have learned a lot about how the world works because of the progress of science and philosophy

50
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:35,000
but we still talk a language that is handed down to us by Aristotle, Spinoza, and Leibniz.

51
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Now people have tried to fix the language -- here is Bertrand Russell, a more modern philosopher

52
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
trying to point out that the very idea of Cause and Effect

53
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
is no longer fundamental in our understanding of the world.

54
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:56,000
He says, "The Law of Causality, I believe, Like much that passes muster among philosophers, is a relic of a bygone age. Surviving, like the monarchy..."

55
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:02,000
He couldn't resist, right? Like, I'm just the messenger here. This is not -- I'm not saying this --

56
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:08,000
"Surviving, like the monarchy, only because it is erroneously supposed to do no harm."

57
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Now many people would be surprised to hear the news that science has done away with the Principle of Cause and Effect.

58
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
But I'm not gonna undo that -- it's actually true! It's not that there is no such thing as cause and effect.

59
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:25,000
It's that it is not a fundamental principle of how reality works.

60
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
It is a very useful helpful way of thinking it about things at a macroscopic --

61
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
what we will call an emergent -- level of reality.

62
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
But it is nowhere to be found in the most fundamental laws of physics.

63
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
And that difference puts us in the position of Wile E Coyote, a famous American cartoon character.

64
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,000
I don't know how popular the Roadrunner is in uh in the UK? Good.

65
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
So Wile E Coyote you will remember if you're of the right age

66
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:55,000
had this thing that he did every single episode where he would run off a cliff.

67
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,000
And because it's cartoon physics -- not either Aristotelian or Newtonian physics -- he would not fall down

68
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
until he noticed that he was not standing on anything.

69
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
He would look around, and then oh! and then he would fall down, okay

70
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
We are all Wile E Coyote in some sense and in the following sense --

71
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:21,000
This cliff, this solid surface that we used to be walking on, is our old fashioned,

72
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Aristotelian et al., notion of Causes and Effects and Purposes and Meanings in the world.

73
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:35,000
We have left that behind. The fundamental rules of nature, according to our best scientific understanding, don't work that way

74
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:42,000
but we haven't adapted. We still speak a language as if Cause and Effect, Purpose and Goals,

75
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
and Reasons Why are the fundamental way the world works.

76
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
So one of our challenges should be to reconcile the

77
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:55,000
deep down vocabulary of the world given to us by physics and modern science

78
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:02,000
with what philosophers call the Manifest Image -- the immediately accessible view of the world we have.

79
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:08,000
After all, physicists love to make fun of Aristotle and say how wrong he was, but look --

80
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,000
the book does stop moving, right?  That's not a mistake!

81
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
That is an accurate way of talking about the world.

82
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Cause and Effect are accurate useful ways of talking about the world. You can be accurate without being fundamental.

83
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:26,000
Our goal is to show how at different levels of analysis -- different levels of squinting at the world --

84
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
reveal different rules, regularities, and even vocabularies for speaking.

85
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,000
So I like to trace the origin of this shift from Aristotle to Bertrand Russell for example

86
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:47,000
It's actually a long series of very very interesting thoughts and experiments over hundreds of years

87
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
between Aristotle and let's say Isaac Newton.  I like to give some credit to this guy Ibn Sina who sometimes

88
00:07:52,000 --> 00:08:01,000
Romanized or Latinized as "Avicenna". He was a Persian polymath in the Islamic Golden Age in around the Year 1000

89
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:06,000
and as a modern day theoretical physicist I find Ibn Sina extremely annoying.

90
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
because his day job was he was a doctor

91
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
he was his interests were in health and human anatomy and he wrote many books about the human body

92
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
and on weekends he invented new fundamental laws of physics.

93
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:23,000
We can't do that anymore, it's more work now. The low-hanging fruit has been picked.

94
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:29,000
So Ibn Sina was thinking about Aristotelian notions of motion and so forth

95
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and what he pointed out for the very first time was that this idea that the book stops moving

96
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
shouldn't be thought of as the book returning to its natural state of motion

97
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
because if the book was not on the table here in a room,

98
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
but rather out in space -- like the spacecraft -- if it were in the vacuum

99
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:56,000
the book would just keep moving at a constant velocity. What we now call Conservation of Momentum or Conservation of Inertia.

100
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Now Ibn Sina didn't like this idea. He didn't even like the idea of a vacuum.

101
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:03,000
In the year 1000 we weren't sure whether there were any vacua ("vacuums")

102
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
so he put forward this idea, but it was seized upon by later thinkers.

103
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:14,000
There's no such thing as natural motion versus being pushed -- the natural motion of things is just to keep moving

104
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
you don't need to keep things pushed.

105
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And of course Galileo helped develop this idea. He pointed out that if you think of the

106
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
fundamental starting point of motion as constant motion with the uniform velocity,

107
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,000
and then put in things like friction and dissipation and air resistance afterward,

108
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
you get a much more accurate, precise, quantitative feeling for what happens in the world.

109
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So the secret thing that sneaks in is not just a new way to torture first-year physics

110
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
students with inclined planes and pulleys and so forth.

111
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
There's a fundamentally new way of looking at the world.

112
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
The world is not made of Motions that have a Mover, Effects that have Causes

113
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
There are things that are doing their thing according to some rigorous

114
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:07,000
mathematical laws of physics, some conservation laws very often, such as conservation of momentum.

115
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
And of course once Isaac Newton came along and put together his theory

116
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:18,000
of physics and motion and mechanics we had a much deeper version of Conservation

117
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:23,000
what you could call Conservation of Information. And even though it's sort of in some implicit

118
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:29,000
sense due to Isaac Newton, it was really Pierre Simone Laplace who stuck his neck out and really

119
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:35,000
understood the implications of Newtonian mechanics. Laplace points out the following thing.

120
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
Let's imagine that we believed Newton's laws of motion -- which they did at the time --

121
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:47,000
and in fact I'll point out we updated them since then but it's not a fundamentally -- not in a way that really changes this particular analysis.

122
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Laplace says look, think of two billiard balls bouncing into each other and scattering off.

123
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:58,000
Now he takes these in the tradition of Ibn Sina and Galileo and so forth to be physicist billiard balls

124
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
They make no noise, they have no air resistance, are on a frictionless surface.

125
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:08,000
So they scatter off and they go their own way. The traditional question we would ask of our students is, if you

126
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:14,000
gave me the information at the start of the experiment -- so where the balls were, and how they were moving --

127
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:20,000
I could tell you -- I could solve the equations and tell you how they would evolve thereafter.

128
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:24,000
But what Laplace points out is that in fact you could give me the

129
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:29,000
information about what the billiard balls are doing at any moment in time.

130
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:35,000
You could not only tell what's going to happen next, according to Newton's laws, you could say what did happen before that.

131
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:44,000
The information necessary to tell you what's happening in those billiard balls is contained equally well in every moment of their existence.

132
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:51,000
So he says imagine a vast intellect. Later commentators thought that "vast intellect" was insufficiently sexy,

133
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:55,000
so they said imagine a Demon -- which we now call Laplace's Demon --

134
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:01,000
and of course if we had been at 20th or a 21st century commentator would have been imagine a really big computer.

135
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,000
That knew the position and the velocity of everything in the universe and all the laws of physics

136
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:11,000
and had apparently infinite calculational abilities -- computational capacity.

137
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:19,000
To this vast intellect, Laplace says, there is no difference between the present the past and the future.

138
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:26,000
The future and the past are equally transparent and known as the present is, because they are determined by the Laws of Nature.

139
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:33,000
So this sets up a whole long, centuries-long debate about Determinism and Freewill and etc

140
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:38,000
But there's something deeper that gets glossed over sometimes and it is worth bringing out

141
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:44,000
It's not that anything goes, but the vocabulary has changed.

142
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Aristotle would have said if things are moving, there's something moving them. There's a Cause or a Reason Why they are moving.

143
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Laplace says they just obey equations. What that means is that rather than a cause-and-effect relationship,

144
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:02,000
we have patterns in the universe. Think about the integers, right?

145
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
The number zero, one, two, three, and also negative minus one, minus two, minus three...

146
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:13,000
There's a pattern there. If you tell me any one number, if you say "three", I know what the number before that was.

147
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:17,000
It was two. I know what the number after that's gonna be, it's gonna be four.

148
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:25,000
But I don't think that three is the cause of four or vice-versa. There's just a pattern that relates all those numbers to each other.

149
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
Laplace is saying the laws of physics are like that.

150
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:32,000
It's not that there is an impulse, an enchantment, a Guiding Force

151
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:37,000
it's just there's a pattern that says if-this-then-that-and-vice-versa

152
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:43,000
and this way of thinking about how the world works at a deep level is something we have yet to truly absorb.

153
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:47,000
Of course Laplace was not right. He didn't know about the true laws of physics,

154
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:52,000
he thought that Newton's laws were more or less correct he had every reason to believe that was true

155
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:58,000
but since then we have Relativity and Quantum Mechanics and so forth, so we have a better idea now of what the laws fundamentally are.

156
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
In fact, one of the bold claims I want to make

157
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:06,000
-- and you're willing to disbelieve me if you want, but you would be incorrect --

158
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:13,000
is that the Laws of Physics underlying everyday life are today completely known.

159
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:21,000
Here, I put on a slide just in case. This sounds very much like one of those incredibly

160
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:26,000
dopey pretentious statements that scientists have made for hundreds of years right? Scientists, especially

161
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:33,000
physicists -- my tribe -- are very very famous for saying, "You know any day now, we'll have it all figured out,

162
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:38,000
we'll have the Theory of Everything." This was especially common in the late 19th Century.

163
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:43,000
People were saying, "Yes, we have you know Mechanics and Thermodynamics and Electromagnetism,

164
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:47,000
any day now we'll have all the physics figured out."

165
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:51,000
And then of course it all went to Hell because they invented Relativity and Quantum Mechanics and so forth.

166
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:57,000
I'm not saying that. I'm not making any statement whatsoever about how close we are to

167
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
understanding all of the laws of physics or everything we are made of.

168
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:06,000
What I'm saying is we know some of the laws of physics some of the things that we are made of,

169
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and more importantly the regime that we do understand -- the domain of validity of our current knowledge --

170
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:20,000
includes everything in this room.  Includes everything that you experience in the everyday regime of your life,

171
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,000
as long as your everyday regime is not that of an experimental particle physicist.

172
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:30,000
You are made of atoms. You are made of particles: electrons, protons, neutrons.

173
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Those protons and neutrons are made of quarks: 'up' and 'down' quarks

174
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:42,000
And these particles feel forces and there's basically four forces that are relevant here: gravity pulling everything together,

175
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,000
electromagnetism pulling together unlike charges and pushing away like charges.

176
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:53,000
There's a strong nuclear force that binds those quarks together to make the proton and the neutron.

177
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:58,000
And there's a weak nuclear force which is almost irrelevant except it helps the Sun shine so that's kind of important.

178
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:05,000
The weak nuclear force converts protons to neutrons and vice versa by spitting out a particle called a neutrino.

179
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:12,000
Okay, so four particles: electron, up quark, down quark, neutrino. Four forces: gravity, electromagnetism, strong, and weak.

180
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:19,000
... and that's it. We know there are other particles. There are muons. There are top quarks etc

181
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
You can argue over the exact cutoff for everyday life, okay?

182
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:31,000
But you're made of these particles and everything you see with your eyes, touch with your fingers, taste with your tongue

183
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:41,000
made of these particles. And the statement I want to make is that understanding of what you're made of -- the particles and the forces, the laws that they obey --

184
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:47,000
is true. It's not gonna go away. It's not like Epicycles or

185
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:53,000
Phlogiston or Caloric, these ideas that we had in the past that we showed later were completely wrong

186
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:02,000
It's an idea that a thousand years from now or a million years from now we're still going to believe that these particles exist, that the rules that we now know are

187
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:07,000
accurate ways of talking about how they behave inside you. We might get a deeper understanding

188
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:12,000
you might realize that Space and Time themselves aren't fundamental.

189
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:18,000
We certainly don't know how to take these laws and build them up to make Biology and Chemistry and Economics or anything like that.

190
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:26,000
But at this particular level -- What are the particles and forces that you and I are made out of, what are the equations that tell us how they behave?

191
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:33,000
We know that. And I know that you don't believe me because I'm just showing you a cartoon. You're thinking to yourself

192
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:39,000
I'm not gonna believe this until I see the equation. So here you go

193
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:44,000
This is the equation. I'm very grateful to the Royal Institution for giving me these six hours, so I can explain

194
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:51,000
all of the terms in this equation in great detail. But that's okay, you don't need to know all the details.

195
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:58,000
As you see from the labels on the equation this is a single equation that more or less is the information

196
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:04,000
that the modern-day version of Laplace's Demon would need to tell you what happens in the world.

197
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:09,000
This is the answer to the question, you tell me what the configuration of stuff in the world is right now

198
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:14,000
this equation tells you what it will be a little bit in the future, what it was a little bit in the past.

199
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:20,000
It's a quantum mechanical equation, so if you observe the system,

200
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:24,000
one of the things about quantum mechanics is you can only predict probabilities not certainties.

201
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:30,000
But this is the equation that tells you what those probabilities are. It includes Quantum Mechanics, Spacetime,

202
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
all the matter particles that we're made of as well as all the forces that we know about and of course the Higgs field

203
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:41,000
Lurking in the background that we finally had evidence for back in 2012.

204
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:49,000
What you don't see in this picture is anything that Aristotle would recognize as a Final Cause

205
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:54,000
or anything that Leibniz would recognize as the Reason Why a certain event is happening.

206
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:59,000
The language being spoken here is that of patterns and differential equations,

207
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:05,000
not of causes, purposes, meanings, and there's no values here. There's no judgments.

208
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:09,000
This equation -- what we call the Core Theory of Physics --

209
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:13,000
which has all the particle physics and also all the gravity that we know about.

210
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:18,000
The Core Theory doesn't pass judgment on you or me.  It doesn't tell us what is right from wrong.

211
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:23,000
It just tells you what is going to happen. Now even though I showed you the equation, you might still not be happy because you say,

212
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:29,000
"Well I only trust equations that can fit on a T-shirt." So I have the experimental evidence that

213
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:38,000
the Core Theory can fit on a T-shirt. We're in good shape now. I know what you're thinking. I've given this talk before, different forms.

214
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:43,000
You're thinking, "Fine, you guys, you physicists, you have your particles and your forces.

215
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:48,000
But it's just the same kind of hubris to say that we're not going to discover new

216
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:55,000
particles and forces that you don't know about yet. How do you know that there's not new particles, Mr. Smartypants Physicist?"

217
00:19:55,000 --> 00:20:00,000
And of course, there's two answers for that. One is it's almost certainly true that there are

218
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,000
new particles and forces that we've not yet discovered.

219
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:10,000
Remember, all I'm claiming is that we've discovered the particles and forces relevant to our everyday lives.

220
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:15,000
As a working theoretical physicist, I certainly hope there are new particles and forces in understanding,

221
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:21,000
I'm just saying that whatever we discover along those lines is not going to affect your Biology.

222
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:24,000
It's not gonna affect your Psychology.

223
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:29,000
It's not gonna affect the motion of the particles that do make up you that we know about right now.

224
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:33,000
So how do we know that? That's a very grandiose claim.

225
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:40,000
It's one that we really could not have made in years past. It turns out to be a very specific feature of the way

226
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:46,000
that this equation works. It is based on the principles of what we call Quantum Field Theory.

227
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:52,000
Field Theory is the idea that what you think is a particle like an electron or a photon is really a

228
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:59,000
vibration in a field filling all of space. Why does it look like a particle instead of looking like a field?

229
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:04,000
That's where the Quantum comes in. Quantum says that when you look at these things that make up the world

230
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,000
they come to you in discrete packets of stuff. We call those packets of stuff "particles."

231
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:14,000
So Quantum Field Theory says the world is made of fields, but Quantum Mechanics

232
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:18,000
gives us the rules for observing them so we can talk about them in terms of particles.

233
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:23,000
And then it goes on to draw implications from that idea.

234
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:29,000
So here's one simple implication of Quantum Field Theory called Crossing Symmetry

235
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that's usually taught as a little technical tool in Quantum Field Theory, but it actually has extremely profound implications.

236
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:43,000
So let's say we imagine that there is a particle or a force or a field that we haven't yet found

237
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:48,000
in our experiments that might in fact play an important role in human biology or neuroscience.

238
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
How would that work?

239
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:57,000
Well the first thing that a physicist would do given the proposition that there's a new particle is start drawing these pictures

240
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:02,000
what are called Feynman Diagrams. Named after Richard Feynman, my predecessor at Caltech.

241
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:06,000
My most famous accomplishment as a physicist is I sit at the desk that Richard Feynman used to sit at.

242
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:11,000
It is the desk given to the most senior theoretical physicist at Caltech

243
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:18,000
who is not senior enough to get a brand new desk when they get there. So I got that one.

244
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:24,000
These Feynman diagrams do two things: they number one show us what can happen. They are pictures of actual processes.

245
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:30,000
So the diagram on the left, you have an ordinary particle at the bottom. Let's say a proton.

246
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:34,000
At the top, the red line is some new particle, some particle that we've hypothesized

247
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:39,000
and we're imagining there's some new interaction or some old interaction

248
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
maybe electromagnetism or the weak nuclear force

249
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:46,000
via which this new particle can interact with the particle that we know about -- the proton --

250
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:52,000
so you read the diagram from left to right it says that the new particle comes in,

251
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:57,000
a proton comes in, and they scatter off of each other by exchanging some Boson --

252
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:01,000
some photon or some new Boson that we haven't heard about okay

253
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:04,000
Number one, the diagram tells you that can happen,

254
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,000
but number two, there are rules for attaching numbers to these diagrams.

255
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:14,000
So if you're a graduate student in physics this diagram will strike fear into your heart, because you go, "oh no

256
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:19,000
I have to calculate a scattering amplitude." And the diagram lets you do that. It tells you the probability

257
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
the two particles will come in and scatter off.

258
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:27,000
Now Crossing Symmetry is a feature of Quantum Field Theory that says

259
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:30,000
given this diagram given some new particle

260
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:35,000
they could in principle interact with -- an old particle that I know I have inside me --

261
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:42,000
I can rotate the diagram by 90 degrees and I get a new diagram that is a little bit different when I rotate it.

262
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:46,000
You know time goes from left to right in these diagrams

263
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:52,000
There's this, there's a little technical rule that if a line gets flipped from going left to right from to going right to left

264
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:57,000
I exchange a particle with an antiparticle. We're not at that level of detail here,

265
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:03,000
but the point is that rotated diagram gives me a new process with the same probability --

266
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:07,000
the same quantum amplitude -- as the old process has.

267
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:10,000
In other words, if the thing on the left can happen

268
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:13,000
where the new particle and the old particle scatter off each other,

269
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:17,000
then the thing on the right can happen where two existing particles --

270
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:24,000
two protons or two electrons or something like that -- can annihilate into each other and

271
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:30,000
create this new particle. You can produce the new particle. It can't hide from you.

272
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:36,000
And this idea that we smash particles together and look to see what comes out and hope that new particles comes out

273
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:42,000
that is what particle physicists do! We've been smashing particles together for decades

274
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:48,000
Electrons and other electrons, electrons and positrons, protons and protons, protons and antiprotons,

275
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:55,000
neutrons... We've smashed everything together that we have in the Core Theory of particle physics. We've seen what comes out

276
00:24:55,000 --> 00:25:00,000
That's how we discovered the Higgs boson at the Large Hadron Collider in 2012

277
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:06,000
Is it possible that there are particles that exist that haven't yet been produced in this way?

278
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:09,000
Sure. But we know what that would mean.

279
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:15,000
Either they interact with ordinary matter so weakly that you can't make them

280
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:20,000
when you smash literally billions of particles together and none of these new particles are created.

281
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
That's possible.

282
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:28,000
But if that's possible then they're not gonna play an important role in you. They're -- you can't make them

283
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:33,000
They're just irrelevant to the processes that describe the atoms and the molecules inside you.

284
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:37,000
The other possibility is that you do make them, but then they quickly decay away.

285
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:40,000
That's what happens with the Higgs boson for example.

286
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:44,000
The Higgs boson you make it then it decays away in one zeptosecond.

287
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:51,000
A zeptosecond is ... a very short period of time. Ten to the minus 21 seconds.

288
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:57,000
So we say we've discovered the Higgs boson at CERN. We've never seen a Higgs boson

289
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:04,000
We've seen the thing the Higgs bosons decay into and if that's the way that these particles have avoided being seen

290
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:11,000
then again, they're not relevant to you and me. If you did have any in your brain, they would decay away. In a zeptosecond. Or less.

291
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:16,000
This is why we can make this kind of statement about our knowledge of the laws of physics underlying everyday life.

292
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:21,000
It's certainly -- you're welcome to imagine other new particles and forces.

293
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:27,000
But if they were there, and they were strongly interacting enough with you and me to be relevant to our everyday lives

294
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:34,000
we would have seen them already and we do not. So we know what you and I are made of.

295
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:42,000
What is remaining to do is to match this underlying Core Theory equation to the everyday life that we see

296
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:45,000
And that's where this principle called Emergence comes in.

297
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:53,000
That we have different vocabularies, different stories we can tell about the world at different levels of detail.

298
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:57,000
The story of the Core Theory with the particles the electrons and the Quantum Fields bumping into each other.

299
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:05,000
That's the microscopic version of reality. Our best current microscopic description. We may in the future do get even deeper layers,

300
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:08,000
but the layer that we have right now won't go away.

301
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:15,000
And that microscopic description is a story of Particles, Fields, Differential Equations.

302
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:20,000
The macroscopic world that we are familiar with in our everyday lives that Aristotle knew about

303
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:25,000
speaks a completely different language. There is dissipation, there's Cause and Effect.

304
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:30,000
There's a natural state for things to move in, there are reasons why

305
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:35,000
things happen rather than not happen and much of this is due to this first

306
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:40,000
item on the list here, the arrow of time. The difference between past and future.

307
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:44,000
The arrow of time is something that is absolutely central to how we think about the world.

308
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:48,000
It is so central that you don't notice it is a thing.

309
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:53,000
Aristotle -- who wrote books on absolutely everything from metaphysics to drama --

310
00:27:53,000 --> 00:22:56,000
never talked about the arrow of time.

311
00:22:56,000 --> 00:27:59,000
Of course the past is different from the future. That's just an obvious thing.

312
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:02,000
What is it -- what are you even asking that question about?

313
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,000
But there's no arrow of time in the Core Theory equation.

314
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:11,000
There was no arrow of time in Newton's equations for describing the world.

315
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:16,000
The best since the time of Isaac Newton the best ways we have of describing the world at the most fundamental

316
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:21,000
microscopic level do not distinguish between past and future in any way.

317
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:27,000
Despite that the world in which we live obviously does distinguish between the past and future in many ways.

318
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:31,000
We remember what happened yesterday. We don't remember the future

319
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:39,000
I hope nobody here remembers the future. You can make choices right? So like right now you could decide

320
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:44,000
that you think this is the most boring lecture you've ever heard you can leave you don't need to be here for the next half

321
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:50,000
of the lecture. But you cannot right now decide not to have come to the lecture.

322
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:54,000
You cannot make a decision that affects the past, right?

323
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
There's an asymmetry of influence. Where does that come from

324
00:28:56,000 --> 00:29:01,000
if the underlying laws treat the past and future symmetrically?

325
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:04,000
Well, it all comes down to this egg breaking.

326
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:07,000
If you understand the egg breaking, you understand why all these things are true.

327
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:13,000
This egg breaking illustrates the increase of Entropy or disorderliness -- the Second Law of Thermodynamics,

328
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:18,000
(which is going to be a theme of the Royal Institution's Advent Calendar)

329
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:24,000
Entropy increases. You clean your room, you leave it to its own devices, your room gets messier over time

330
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:29,000
that's a fundamental law of physics. Of course you can clean it again.

331
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
But that's because your room is not a closed system.

332
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:34,000
In an isolated system or in the universe as a whole

333
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:38,000
entropy increases the universe becomes more disorderly.

334
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:41,000
The reason why is because there are more ways to be

335
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:45,000
low-- more ways to be high entropy than to be low entropy.

336
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:49,000
You give me an orderly arrangement like an unbroken egg, it is easy to break it.

337
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:54,000
There's a lot more ways to arrange the molecules in the egg in the form of a broken egg or scrambled eggs

338
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:58,000
than there are in a very delicately chosen arrangement of the unbroken egg.

339
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:04,000
That's half of the reason why there is an arrow of time. There's more ways to be high entropy to be low entropy.

340
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:10,000
But the other half is the universe was low entropy in the past.

341
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:16,000
That's more of a puzzle. Why was the universe lower entropy, more orderly, yesterday than it is today?

342
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:22,000
I can tell you the answer. It's because it was even lower Entropy the day before yesterday.

343
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:27,000
And the reason why that's true is because it was even lower entropy the day before that

344
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:31,000
and this logic goes back 13.8 billion years to the Big Bang.

345
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:38,000
There's no fundamental arrow of time, just like there's no fundamental arrow of space in the laws of physics.

346
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:44,000
If you were an astronaut doing experiments, there'd be no difference between up down left right forward backward in front of you.

347
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:47,000
You could rotate yourself out there in space it wouldn't make any difference.

348
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,000
Here in this room, there's an arrow of space.

349
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:54,000
If I let go the book it falls. I could predict what direction it's gonna fall in -- it goes down.

350
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:57,000
We don't think that's built into the nature of reality,

351
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:02,000
we think it's because we live in the vicinity of an influential object, namely the Earth.

352
00:31:02,000 --> 00:31:06,000
What I'm telling you is that the arrow of time is exactly the same way.

353
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:13,000
It's tempting, it's natural to think that the difference between past and future is somehow inherent in the nature of reality

354
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:20,000
but it's not. It's because we live in the aftermath of an influential event -- the Big Bang

355
00:31:20,000 --> 00:31:28,000
That had a very low entropy, was a very organised system. We don't know why. If you want to know why -- well no one knows why

356
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:33,000
If you want to think about why I wrote a book that was the first book I wrote, _From Eternity to Here_

357
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:36,000
You can buy that, that's where this figure is from.

358
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:42,000
So what this is revealing to us, is that unlike an Aristotelian view which was teleological --

359
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:48,000
things were directed toward a future goal. They were headed toward going back to their natural state of being.

360
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:52,000
If anything, the macroscopic world is Echinological

361
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:58,000
which is from the Greek words start or beginning. The special state of the universe was where it began.

362
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:03,000
We don't know why it began in such a special state, but since then it's just been winding down.

363
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:08,000
That's all it's been doing. There is no future goal or place toward which we're going.

364
00:32:08,000 --> 00:32:15,000
That increase of entropy is sufficient to explain all the differences between past and future.

365
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:19,000
We can't go through all of them -- that would require another book, or another lecture --

366
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:23,000
but let's think about the idea of a memory or a record

367
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:31,000
Some artifact some feature of the present day that gives us knowledge of the past in a reliable way.

368
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:38,000
So maybe that's literally a memory in your head, maybe it's a photograph maybe you're walking down the street on the sidewalk you see a broken egg

369
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:47,000
I claim that the evidence that you have (there's an egg broken on the sidewalk) gives you different leverage over the past than over the future.

370
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:51,000
You can ask yourself: what does the future of the egg hold?

371
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:56,000
Well many things are possible right? It could just sit there, someone could clean it up, a dog could come by and eat it,

372
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:59,000
it could be washed away.  There's many different possible futures.

373
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:04,000
What was the past of the egg probably like? Well, with very large probability

374
00:33:04,000 --> 00:33:08,000
there was an unbroken egg and somebody dropped it, right?

375
00:33:08,000 --> 00:33:13,000
We can say something much more specific and informative about the past given this evidence of the egg right now

376
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:17,000
than we can about the future. Why is that?

377
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:22,000
Again, if all you knew were the deepest laws of physics that equation I showed you or Newton's laws or whatever

378
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:26,000
your ability to extrapolate toward the past and future would be identical.

379
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:31,000
You have some knowledge of the worlds present state, but it is incomplete

380
00:33:31,000 --> 00:33:36,000
You have this macroscopic configuration of the egg but you don't know what all the atoms and molecules are doing.

381
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:44,000
The number of things the egg could possibly do toward the future is exactly equal to the number of things that could have been doing in the past

382
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:48,000
if all you know is the fundamental laws of physics. But you know something else.

383
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:52,000
You know that the early universe had a low entropy. You know that our past --

384
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:57,000
something that philosophers have sadly labelled the Past Hypothesis -- (which is most boring label I've ever heard)

385
00:33:57,000 --> 00:34:02,000
but the Past Hypothesis says the universe started with low entropy

386
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:08,000
and that provides an anchor that provides an asymmetry between what we know about the past and what we know about the future.

387
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:10,000
So you have not only the present information, but also

388
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:16,000
that past anchor and that lets you infer features of what actually happened in the past

389
00:34:16,000 --> 00:34:19,000
If you have an egg broken on the sidewalk there used to be an unbroken egg

390
00:34:19,000 --> 00:34:27,000
If you have a photograph of you ten years old wearing a red sweater, you probably were wearing a red sweater that day.

391
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:33,000
We all know that -- we've all heard, right, that there's this general tendency of the universe to wind down and

392
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:40,000
evolve toward its heat death over time. And there is in my country, there's a controversy over

393
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:46,000
what is called Creationism. Some people think that if you think that there's a fundamental feature of the world

394
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:52,000
where things just go to more more disorderly states that is incompatible with the

395
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:47,000
appearance over a cosmological history of things like you and me.

396
00:34:47,000 --> 00:35:05,000
Because we're not low entropy. How is it possible that such highly organized things like you and me could just pop into existence

397
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:09,000
in a world that is generally becoming more and more disorganized?

398
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:15,000
And scientists have an immediate glib answer to this: the Earth and its biosphere is not a closed system

399
00:35:15,000 --> 00:35:21,000
It's not isolated. You can clean your room. There are things called refrigerators.

400
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:25,000
If you put your bottle of champagne in the refrigerator its entropy will go down as it cools, okay?

401
00:35:25,000 --> 00:25:30,000
So therefore there's no contradiction with the second law of thermodynamics that low entropy things

402
00:25:30,000 --> 00:35:35,000
like you and me came to be as the universe expanded and cooled.

403
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:40,000
On the other hand, just because you can clean your room doesn't mean you will clean your room.

404
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:44,000
Some of you may have experienced this with children or even yourselves

405
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:50,000
So the fact that the Earth is not a closed system allows for the appearance of organized systems like you and me

406
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:56,000
but it doesn't explain why it happened. The explanation is of course incomplete. We don't know the full answer.

407
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:02,000
But it relies on the fact that there's a difference between simplicity and complexity.

408
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:08,000
There's an axis if you like between simple systems and complex systems;

409
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:13,000
another axis between low entropy organized things and high entropy disorganized things.

410
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:18,000
Being disorganized does not mean being simple or being complex.

411
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:24,000
Think about one of my favorite examples: mixing cream into coffee. On the left, you have a low entropy situation

412
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:27,000
all the cream's on the top, all the coffee's on the bottom.

413
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:35,000
As time goes on, the cream mixes into the coffee. On the right, you have a high entropy situation everything is mixed together

414
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:39,000
That's a natural flow of time from past to future.

415
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:43,000
But think about the system on the left. It is very simple.

416
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:46,000
Here the technical definition of simplicity vs. complexity is:

417
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:52,000
"How long do you have to talk to me to describe the system in full detail?" How many bytes of information do you need?

418
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:59,000
On the left, all the cream's on the top all the coffee's on the bottom. It's very simple.

419
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:04,000
On the right, everything is mixed together. It's also very simple.

420
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:10,000
It's in between, it's where those tendrils of cream and coffee are mixing together in some intricate fractal pattern

421
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:19,000
that's when things look complex. So there's a natural tendency as the universe ages for entropy to increase

422
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:24,000
but at the same time complexity first increases and then decreases.

423
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:31,000
It's not only that complex systems are allowed to come into existence when entropy is increasing.

424
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:37,000
In a very real sense, they do come into existence because entropy is increasing

425
00:37:37,000 --> 00:37:44,000
or at least they can maintain themselves, they can maintain structure and order and self-repair

426
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:49,000
because we're in a very low entropy universe that is only gradually becoming more and more disorderly.

427
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:55,000
And it's not just cups of coffee and cream that this is true for this is true for the universe.

428
00:37:55,000 --> 00:37:59,000
So here is the history of our observable universe in a very brief presentation

429
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:04,000
It started out -- we don't know what happened at the Big Bang, but one second after the Big Bang we know

430
00:38:04,000 --> 00:38:09,000
what the universe was like. It was hot, dense, and smooth. That's it.

431
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:15,000
That's a very simple explanation a very simple description. I didn't take that many bytes to give it to you.

432
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:22,000
As the universe expands and cools, it becomes increasingly lumpier because gravity pulls things together

433
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:27,000
So a few hundred thousand years after the Big Bang we get the cosmic background radiation

434
00:38:27,000 --> 00:38:30,000
A snapshot of what the universe looked like when it first became transparent

435
00:38:30,000 --> 00:38:36,000
Tiny variations in density from place to place, but still pretty smooth

436
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:40,000
Now fourteen billion years after the Big Bang the universe is very complicated. 

437
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:48,000
We've formed galaxies and stars and planets and Biospheres and lecture halls. It's a very complicated part of the universe

438
00:38:48,000 --> 00:38:53,000
But we can keep going. We discovered in 1998 that the universe is not only expanding, but accelerating

439
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:56,000
It's expanding faster and faster, which means it's never gonna stop

440
00:38:56,000 --> 00:39:01,000
According to our best current theories what will happen is the stars will burn out

441
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:09,000
10 to the 15 years 1 quadrillion years from now the last star will stop shining - Sorry

442
00:39:09,000 --> 00:39:15,000
All those stars are gonna fall into black holes and Stephen Hawking taught us in 1970s that even

443
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:21,000
black holes don't last forever. They give off radiation. So 10 to the 100 years from now

444
00:39:21,000 --> 00:39:26,000
What used to be called a Google before the search engine took over the term 10 to the 100 years from now

445
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:31,000
There'll be nothing left in the observable universe it will literally be nothing but empty space

446
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:36,000
Nothing but empty space is very simple, but it is very high entropy.

447
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:40,000
Everything's very far apart, so there's lots of different arrangements for the things that do exist in the universe

448
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:46,000
So the entropy of this universe just increases, but the complexity first increases and then decreases

449
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:53,000
This is a natural robust generic way that complexity can come into being

450
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:58,000
What we want to do -- that's the easy part -- what we want to do is take that natural robust simple story and apply it

451
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:05,000
to the real evolution of complicated structures like life here on Earth.

452
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:12,000
So for me the epiphany was one day I was taking a plane ride to a conference and I was actually interested in the origin of life

453
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:18,000
I was reading a paper, a technical paper on the relationship between physics and the origin of life

454
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:24,000
So I'm sitting there reading my paper the guy in the plane seat next to me starts talking

455
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:28,000
And you know as a theoretical physicist and cosmologist you meet a lot of people who have theories

456
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:34,000
About the universe that they would like to explain to you.

457
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:38,000
So this guy looks at my paper, and he says, "Oh yes, I'm familiar with that work. In fact

458
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:45,000
I can tell you the purpose of life." Like okay, long plane ride ahead lay it on me.

459
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:49,000
He said the purpose of life is to hydrogenate carbon dioxide

460
00:40:49,000 --> 00:40:55,000
Not what I expected his theory to be. Turns out the guy sitting next to me was

461
00:40:55,000 --> 00:41:04,000
Dr. Michael Russell of the Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena one of the world's leading researchers on the origin of life

462
00:41:04,000 --> 00:41:09,000
What are the odds? Well turns out we were going to the same conference so it's not completely a coincidence

463
00:41:09,000 --> 00:41:12,000
Everything happens for a reason, remember

464
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:18,000
So Mike Russell's theory which is not universally accepted. We don't know how life began

465
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:21,000
there are different competing ideas. Mike Russell's theory -- he has one of the good ideas

466
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:25,000
His idea is that in certain environments of the early Earth

467
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:32,000
were in a low entropy configuration. In particular all the carbon atoms were in the form of carbon dioxide

468
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:35,000
which happens to be relatively low entropy

469
00:41:35,000 --> 00:41:40,000
Given all the water and hydrogen around them, they could be in the form of methane, CH4

470
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:43,000
That would be a higher entropy configuration.

471
00:41:43,000 --> 00:41:50,000
In some sense, to be a little bit poetic about it, it "wants" to be in the form of methane. That would be higher entropy.

472
00:41:50,000 --> 00:42:00,000
But there is no simple chemical reaction that goes from carbon dioxide to methane while increasing the entropy all along there's a barrier in between

473
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
You need to first lower the entropy to get to the higher entropy state

474
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:09,000
So Mike Russell's idea is that even though there's no simple chemical reaction

475
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:13,000
There is a network of complex chemical reactions that could do the trick

476
00:42:13,000 --> 00:42:17,000
and that in the right circumstances that could happen and be self-sustaining

477
00:42:17,000 --> 00:42:25,000
and that self-sustaining metabolism could then break free of its original environment and become the precursor to life

478
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:30,000
So it's more than just a pretty story. He actually made a prediction.

479
00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:37,000
He's a geologist by training and he said, you know if this is true there must exist under the ocean floor a certain kind of geological formation

480
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:45,000
warm alkaline hydrothermal event -- hydrothermal vents where this kind of chemistry is going on

481
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:49,000
and after he made the prediction they found them. This is what you live for in science.

482
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:56,000
This is a picture of the Lost City hydrothermal formation deep underneath the Atlantic Ocean in the Mid-Atlantic Ridge

483
00:42:56,000 --> 00:42:59,000
It's been it lasts for tens of thousands of years

484
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:04,000
So Mike Russell and other people think that maybe this is the kind of place where life began

485
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:10,000
We don't know -- I'm not pushing this theory necessarily -- what I'm pointing out is that rather than saying

486
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:15,000
"But entropy increases why should something as complex as life ever come to be?"

487
00:43:15,000 --> 00:43:22,000
The -- it's very very plausible that the appearance of life depended on the fact that entropy tends to increase

488
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:28,000
so you actually find the complexity depends on entropy increasing and vice versa

489
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:34,000
The reason why entropy could increase in this system is only because you had a complex network of reactions

490
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:37,000
And that of course continues to the present day.

491
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:40,000
We live in an open system. We live here on Earth.

492
00:43:40,000 --> 00:43:46,000
You ask yourself, "What good is the Sun? What does the Sun do for us here on Earth?"

493
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:50,000
You might think, well we get energy from the Sun. But that's not quite right.

494
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:54,000
It's true that we get energy from the Sun. But we give the same amount of energy back to the universe

495
00:43:54,000 --> 00:43:59,000
We radiate back into the universe the same amount of energy that we get from solar radiation

496
00:43:59,000 --> 00:44:05,000
The difference is that for every one photon of light we get from the Sun -- visible light --

497
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:10,000
we radiate twenty photons of infrared light back to the universe

498
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:17,000
with on average one twentieth of the energy each, but twenty times the entropy

499
00:44:17,000 --> 00:44:23,000
The thing that the Sun gives us is not just energy, but energy in a concentrated low entropy form

500
00:44:23,000 --> 00:44:30,000
and we then photosynthesize, chew our cud, eat our cows, give lectures, write books

501
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:36,000
All of that, all those processes increase the entropy of the universe along the way

502
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:39,000
Then we give it back to the universe in the form of infrared radiation

503
00:44:39,000 --> 00:44:46,000
So we are sustained by increasing entropy. Back in the 1800s, it was a reasonable hypothesis

504
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:51,000
that Life was a Thing. We thought for instance that Heat was a Thing

505
00:44:51,000 --> 00:44:56,000
if you put a hot object next to a cold object they equilibrate -- they come to the same temperature -- so people said

506
00:44:56,000 --> 00:45:02,000
"Well, that's kind of like putting two vessels with a fluid in it
the fluid comes to the same level

507
00:45:02,000 --> 00:45:06,000
there must be a heat fluid that flows from the hot thing to the cold thing."

508
00:45:06,000 --> 00:45:10,000
Not true!  Don't believe anything I just said. Heat is not a fluid; heat is a feature

509
00:45:10,000 --> 00:45:15,000
It is a way of talking about the motions of the atoms and molecules

510
00:45:15,000 --> 00:45:22,000
Life is the same way. Life is not a force or a substance that is in your body and then leaves

511
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:27,000
it is a feature. It is a way of talking about what is happening inside you.

512
00:45:27,000 --> 00:45:30,000
The bad news is that that means it will end someday but we'll get to that in a second.

513
00:45:30,000 --> 00:45:35,000
I have lots of bad news. Don't -- don't worry

514
00:45:35,000 --> 00:45:40,000
The good news is that once this happens once you get life

515
00:45:40,000 --> 00:45:45,000
once you get this chemical reaction that sustains itself and reproduces and wants to keep going

516
00:45:45,000 --> 00:45:49,000
there's this wonderful thing that kicks in called evolution

517
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:55,000
If there's different ways the chemical reaction could arrange itself and some of them were more robust and more likely to survive

518
00:45:55,000 --> 00:45:58,000
Professor Darwin would tell you that's what's going to happen

519
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:03,000
Those more robust ones are likely to dominate the future ecosystem in that particular environment

520
00:46:03,000 --> 00:46:08,000
And what that means is that we can once again change our vocabularies

521
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:16,000
just as we change our vocabulary - going from particles and atoms to eggs and entropy and and time

522
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:21,000
We can change our vocabulary when we start talking about biology and evolution

523
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:27,000
because the new words that creep into our vocabulary are words of purpose and reasons why

524
00:46:27,000 --> 00:46:33,000
Why is it that a giraffe has a very long neck? You could say if you want to be annoying about it,

525
00:46:33,000 --> 00:46:39,000
"Well because of the laws of physics and the initial conditions of the universe, that's why giraffe has its long neck."

526
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:45,000
That's the answer to every question, "why is something true?" Laws of physics and the initial conditions of the universe.

527
00:46:45,000 --> 00:46:51,000
But it's not the only possible answer. In fact, it's not a very sensible answer, right? There's another answer that says,

528
00:46:51,000 --> 00:46:58,000
Well, giraffes had mutations in their genes and some of them got longer necks and those were able to reach

529
00:46:58,000 --> 00:47:04,000
sources of food up on trees that other animals with which they were competing were not able to reach

530
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:09,000
and therefore over successive generations the longer and longer necks survived and flourished

531
00:47:09,000 --> 00:47:15,000
and they were naturally selected to be like that. So in a very real sense the purpose of the giraffes long neck

532
00:47:15,000 --> 00:47:20,000
is to help it reach food sources that it couldn't otherwise reach

533
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:27,000
Where did that purpose come in? Did it did someone put it there? No, it evolved naturally, but that doesn't mean it's fake

534
00:47:27,000 --> 00:47:34,000
It doesn't mean it's an illusion. It is a useful emergent vocabulary for talking about what happens at the macroscopic level

535
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:39,000
And you're probably willing to believe that if I'm just talking about life and biology.

536
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:46,000
It's where it comes to thinking and neuroscience and consciousness that people tend to get off the bus.

537
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:52,000
And of course just like with the origin of life we don't understand the origin of consciousness or what it really is.

538
00:47:52,000 --> 00:47:58,000
What we can do is suggest occasional steps in the history of life which might help us understand

539
00:47:58,000 --> 00:48:03,000
why consciousness became an interesting part of biology.

540
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:10,000
This guy up here the C Elegans nematode kind of flat worm is a little model organism

541
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:15,000
that biologists like to study. In your brain the cells doing the thinking are the neurons

542
00:48:15,000 --> 00:48:20,000
There's something like 85 billion neurons in your brain

543
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:23,000
and different neurons are connected to each other and a big project for modern neuroscience

544
00:48:23,000 --> 00:48:29,000
is studying the connectome -- the way that all the neurons in your brain are connected to each other

545
00:48:29,000 --> 00:48:34,000
We're gonna start with a simpler system. We're going to start with C elegans. We have its connectome

546
00:48:34,000 --> 00:48:39,000
We've mapped it out. C elegans flatworm has exactly 302 neurons

547
00:48:39,000 --> 00:48:43,000
So you can actually count them, and then now you counted them and see how they connect to each other

548
00:48:43,000 --> 00:48:49,000
We're trying to figure out what they're for what they do in the flatworm. There is a paper that came out

549
00:48:49,000 --> 00:48:55,000
I'm not sure if it's correct or not -- theoretical physicist remember. But there's a claim that is very interesting one

550
00:48:55,000 --> 00:49:00,000
that says they can identify one of those neurons whose job it is

551
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:05,000
to tell the nematode whether it's looking at itself or the rest of the world

552
00:49:05,000 --> 00:49:10,000
Is this thing in front of me is this dirt is this water, or is this just my tail right?

553
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:15,000
This this one part of one neuron its job is to sort of

554
00:49:15,000 --> 00:49:21,000
Have a little bit of self-awareness, a little bit of "Well, this is me not the rest of the world"

555
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:25,000
now you can see why if that developed just through the natural fluctuations

556
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:31,000
and mutations of evolution it would be an advantageous thing to keep around. We have much more highly developed

557
00:49:31,000 --> 00:49:35,000
self-awareness and you can imagine other steps along the way.

558
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:41,000
Malcolm McIver who is a mechanical engineer at Northwestern he studies fish and

559
00:49:41,000 --> 00:49:49,000
how in particular fish sense their surroundings. So he points out that a fish with its eyes eyes are very ubiquitous in life

560
00:49:49,000 --> 00:49:55,000
They developed multiple independent times in the course of evolution. But eyes aren't that great if you're underwater

561
00:49:55,000 --> 00:50:01,000
you know you can see tens of meters at most. The attenuation length of light is just not that far and

562
00:50:01,000 --> 00:50:09,000
you're swimming at meters per second. So if you're a fish every time you see something new you have seconds to react to it

563
00:50:09,000 --> 00:50:15,000
maybe a second, right? So the evolutionary pressure is to make a decision really quickly is this

564
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:20,000
food is this friendly, or is this foe and I should run away?

565
00:50:20,000 --> 00:50:24,000
So you don't need to think too much if you're a fish, but when you hop onto land

566
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:29,000
Now the attenuation length of photons is kilometers. You can see off to the horizon. You can see essentially forever

567
00:50:29,000 --> 00:50:35,000
It's possible that you now see something coming long before you need to react

568
00:50:35,000 --> 00:50:42,000
So there's a new evolutionary pressure that starts exerting itself that if you just through the randomness of evolution

569
00:50:42,000 --> 00:50:48,000
develop the ability to contemplate different hypothetical scenarios

570
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:56,000
Then you have a new way of winning the struggle for survival. That is to say, up on land it pays to develop an imagination

571
00:50:56,000 --> 00:51:03,000
Not just an awareness of yourself, but the ability to put yourself hypothetically into different situations and say what is the right thing to do?

572
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:06,000
We don't know if any of this is right, okay?

573
00:51:06,000 --> 00:51:13,000
We don't know how consciousness evolved or even we don't even know the right definition of consciousness, but you can imagine

574
00:51:13,000 --> 00:51:19,000
stories like this will be put together by the progress of biology and neuroscience over the years

575
00:51:19,000 --> 00:51:22,000
The only thing that I really care about I really want to stress

576
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:27,000
is that there's nothing that we know about consciousness that is incompatible with the idea

577
00:51:27,000 --> 00:51:32,000
that we are made of the particles and fields of the core theory obeying that equation

578
00:51:32,000 --> 00:51:43,000
Here I have a picture of my brain. This is actually my it's not my brain it's the skull so I was in it's not to scale either but

579
00:51:43,000 --> 00:51:50,000
I was in a machine MEG Magneto Encephalogram machine and there were

580
00:51:50,000 --> 00:51:55,000
sensors placed on my skull that looked for magnetic fields you've seen fMRI

581
00:51:55,000 --> 00:52:01,000
pictures which are able very very good at locating things spatially in the brain But their time resolution is not very good

582
00:52:01,000 --> 00:52:05,000
An MEG has great time resolution because what happens is

583
00:52:05,000 --> 00:52:11,000
Your brain is made of particles in the core theory in that includes your neurons so when your neurons talk to each other

584
00:52:11,000 --> 00:52:19,000
electrically charged particles race down the neurons and as people who stood at this desk long before me could tell you

585
00:52:19,000 --> 00:52:27,000
when charged particles start moving you create a magnetic field. So this sense is. The magnetic field that those blue, and red splotches are the magnetic

586
00:52:27,000 --> 00:52:33,000
fields the south and north poles coming in and out of my skull when I think a thought

587
00:52:33,000 --> 00:52:39,000
This is just a reminder of something you already know which is that thoughts are associated with real physical things

588
00:52:39,000 --> 00:52:47,000
happening in your brain. They're not abstract things outside our physical bodies there used to be a theory that they were right Rene Descartes

589
00:52:47,000 --> 00:52:57,000
very famously promulgated a theory of mind-body dualism. He was a skeptic and he came up with a "Cogito, ergo sum" argument, I think therefore I am,

590
00:52:57,000 --> 00:53:06,000
I cannot doubt the existence of my mind because it is the thing doing the doubting But he says it's very easy for me to doubt the existence of my body

591
00:53:06,000 --> 00:53:13,000
so if I can doubt the existence of my body but not the existence of my mind they must be two separate things. He wrote books about this became very famous

592
00:53:13,000 --> 00:53:19,000
died a tragic death in the cold, but that's a whole another story, so... There weren't a lot of tenured faculty

593
00:53:19,000 --> 00:53:26,000
positions at the time of Descartes in the 1600s so one had to be nice to potential patrons

594
00:53:26,000 --> 00:53:34,000
So he got to know this young lady Princess Elizabeth of Bohemia. His his aim wasn't very good. They were in exile and didn't have a lot of money

595
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:40,000
Princess Elizabeth and her family, but fortunately Princess Elizabeth was a genius. She was a wonderful thinker very highly educated

596
00:53:40,000 --> 00:53:49,000
She devoured Descartes' books and she didn't agree with what he said. And they struck up a correspondence which is a wonderful thing to read I encourage you to go look up

597
00:53:49,000 --> 00:53:59,000
Princess Elizabeth's correspondence with Descartes and Elizabeth says, "look, this mind-body stuff I just don't know -- you're saying that there's a mind that is literally

598
00:53:59,000 --> 00:54:04,000
Immaterial that has no location in space and yet it clearly

599
00:54:04,000 --> 00:54:10,000
Influences what our bodies do when I talk presumably my mind has something to do with the words, I'm saying

